Then extend to multiple regression:
Sales = f(Advertising, Price, Units)
This is a nice bridge from Excel analytics into machine learning.
6. Moving Average
Now move to time-series analysis.
Business question:
"Daily sales fluctuate heavily. How can management see the underlying trend?"

For a 3-day moving average:
=AVERAGE(F2:F4)
Then drag down:
Day 1   400,000
Day 2   600,000
Day 3   500,000   → MA = 500,000
Day 4   700,000   → MA = 600,000
Day 5   900,000   → MA = 700,000
Also demonstrate:
Data → Data Analysis → Moving Average
Then plot:
Actual Sales vs Moving Average
Explain that moving averages smooth short-term fluctuations and make trends easier to see, though they can lag sudden changes.
Move from Analysis to Decision Making
7. What-If Analysis
Create a simple profit model:
Units Sold       1,000
Selling Price    ₹500
Variable Cost    ₹300
Fixed Cost       ₹100,000
Revenue:
=Units*Price
Profit:
=(Units*(Price-VariableCost))-FixedCost
Current profit:
1000 × (500-300) - 100000 = ₹100,000
Now ask:
"What happens to profit if price changes?"

Use:
Data → What-If Analysis → Data Table
Put different prices:
₹400
₹450
₹500
₹550
₹600
₹650
Excel calculates profit for every price.
Then teach a two-variable Data Table:
Selling Price × Units Sold → Profit
This is excellent for sensitivity analysis.
8. Goal Seek
Reverse the previous problem.
Instead of:
"If I sell 1,000 units, what is my profit?"

Ask:
"How many units must I sell to earn ₹500,000 profit?"

Go to:
Data → What-If Analysis → Goal Seek
Set cell:       Profit
To value:       500000
By changing:    Units Sold
This usually creates an immediate "aha" moment because Excel works backwards from the business target.
Other examples:
What selling price gives 20% margin?

What monthly sales are required to break even?

What interest rate makes EMI ₹50,000?

9. Scenario Manager
Now introduce uncertainty.
Create three scenarios:
Variable	Best Case	Expected	Worst Case
Units	1,500	1,000	700
Price	₹550	₹500	₹450
Variable Cost	₹280	₹300	₹340


Go to:
Data → What-If Analysis → Scenario Manager → Add
Create:
Best Case
Expected Case
Worst Case
Changing cells:
Units + Price + Variable Cost
Result cell:
Profit
Then generate Scenario Summary.
Explain the difference:
Goal Seek: "What input gets me this result?"
Scenario Manager: "What happens under different combinations of assumptions?"
Data Table: "How sensitive is my result to one or two variables?"
Data Preparation & BI
10. Loading Data
Now deliberately give students data outside Excel.
Have three files:
January_Sales.csv
February_Sales.csv
March_Sales.csv
Teach:
Data → Get Data → From File → From Text/CSV
Then:
Load
versus
Transform Data
Explain that Load brings data into Excel, whereas Transform opens Power Query so it can be cleaned before loading.
Also demonstrate importing from:
CSV / TXT → Excel Workbook → Folder → Web → Database
11. Power Query Transformations
This should be one of the most hands-on parts.
Give intentionally dirty data:
" NORTH "
"north"
"North"
"HYDERABAD "
₹12,000
NULL
15/09/26
In Power Query Editor, teach:
Remove Columns → Remove Rows → Remove Duplicates → Replace Values → Trim → Clean → Change Data Type → Split Column → Merge Columns → Fill Down → Group By → Pivot → Unpivot → Merge Queries → Append Queries
Example:
Raw:
Customer_Name = "  ABC RETAIL "
Apply:
Transform → Format → Trim
Result:
ABC RETAIL
Then transform:
north
NORTH
North
to:
North
North
North
The big lesson:
Don't manually clean the same monthly report every month. Build the transformation once and refresh it.

12. PivotTable
Once clean data is loaded:
Insert → PivotTable
Start with a management question:
"Show sales and profit by region and product."

Configure:
Rows        → Region
Columns     → Product
Values      → Sum of Sales
Values      → Sum of Profit
Filters     → Year
Then teach:
Sort → Filter → Value Field Settings → % of Grand Total → Group Dates → Slicers → Timeline → PivotChart
A particularly useful exercise:
"Build an interactive regional sales dashboard without writing a single formula."

Students create:
PivotTables + PivotCharts + Slicers
13. Power Query + PivotTable Together
This is where the pieces connect.
Show this architecture:
CSV Files → Power Query → Clean/Transform → Data Model/Excel → PivotTable → Dashboard
Then add a new month's CSV.
Instead of rebuilding everything:
Data → Refresh All
The dashboard updates.
This demonstrates a basic automated analytics pipeline inside Excel.
14. Live Data Connection and Analysis
End with something more advanced.
Explain the difference between:
Import/Load: Excel stores a copy/snapshot of the data.
Connected data: Excel maintains a connection to an external data source that can be refreshed.
Demonstrate a source such as:
Data → Get Data → From Database → SQL Server
or a suitable web/API source.
Conceptually:
Source System → Power Query/Connection → Excel Data Model → PivotTable/Dashboard
Then show:
Data → Queries & Connections → Properties → Refresh
Depending on the connector and Excel version, refresh behavior can vary, so I would describe this as refreshable/connected analysis rather than implying that every connection is continuously real-time.
